{
  "track": "es",
  "level": "A1",
  "unit": 37,
  "title": "Feelings",
  "description": "Say how you feel, and how other people feel.",
  "explanation": {
    "summary": "Feelings are adjectives, and the ones that describe a temporary state go with estar: estoy feliz, está triste, están aburridos. The adjective changes its ending to match the person it describes.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "The adjectives",
        "body": "El happy one is feliz, and its feminine is feliz as well. The rest change: triste stays the same, aburrido becomes aburrida, enfadado becomes enfadada, tranquilo becomes tranquila, nervioso becomes nerviosa, contento becomes contenta, listo becomes lista, preocupado becomes preocupada and soñoliento becomes soñolienta.",
        "examples": [
          {
            "es": "estoy feliz",
            "en": "I am happy"
          },
          {
            "es": "ella está aburrida",
            "en": "she is bored"
          },
          {
            "es": "están preocupadas",
            "en": "they are worried"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which verb to use",
        "body": "The two verbs for what something is like both take an adjective, and the adjective comes after them. Ser is for what a thing is: el examen es difícil. Estar is for how a thing is now: estoy cansado. For feelings, it is estar that you want.",
        "examples": [
          {
            "es": "estoy tranquilo",
            "en": "I am calm"
          },
          {
            "es": "mi hermano está nervioso",
            "en": "my brother is nervous"
          },
          {
            "es": "la clase es difícil",
            "en": "the class is difficult"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
